1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,500
Our support team is moving customers from a confidential CRM into a new helpdesk.

2
00:00:09,500 --> 00:00:20,000
Gemini could design that integration in seconds — but these customer records are not allowed to leave this machine.

3
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:32,000
I connect the private CRM export and the helpdesk's destination schema.

4
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:45,000
Nothing has left the browser.

5
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:57,000
Gemini Nano runs on this device. It reads the private field names and a few values — locally.

6
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:10,000
EYES builds a Phantom Twin: aliases and fictional rows instead of real ones.

7
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:20,000
Here is exactly what the cloud may see: aliases, fictional examples, the destination. Not my records, not my field names.

8
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:30,000
I approve this one prompt, bound to its hash.

9
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:50,000
Gemini Cloud designs the mapping from the twin alone. Five declarative operations — data, not code.

10
00:01:50,000 --> 00:02:00,000
Every record passes the schema. But my browser checks meaning: this CRM writes dates day-first.

11
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:10,000
Gemini read them month-first — the 3rd of February would become the 2nd of March.

12
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:22,000
I approve one more request: the finding and the rule, no records. Gemini fixes the mapping blind.

13
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:35,000
My browser re-checks it: zero to seven correct records, no regressions.

14
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:50,000
What I take away is a verified, inspectable integration that runs with plain Node — outside the app, with every hash checked.

15
00:02:50,000 --> 00:03:00,000
Local AI for private understanding. Cloud AI for design. Deterministic checks for the truth. Eyes Without a Face.
